+\begin{proposition}[Fixed-surface RM polarizations]
+\label{prop:fixed-surface-rm-polarizations}
+Let~$k$ be a number field and let~$A/k$ be a geometrically simple abelian
+surface.  Let~$F$ be a real quadratic field with maximal order~$\calO_F$.
+
+Fix an isomorphism
+$\iota\colon\calO_F\xrightarrow{\sim}\End_k(A)$ and a principal polarization
+$\lambda\colon A\to A^\vee$ over~$k$.  Write~$\dagger_\lambda$ for its Rosati
+involution and~$\calO_F^{\times,+}$ for the totally positive units.
+
+Every principal polarization over~$k$ on the fixed underlying surface~$A$ is
+uniquely of the form
+\[
+    \lambda_\epsilon=\lambda\circ\iota(\epsilon),
+    \qquad \epsilon\in\calO_F^{\times,+}.
+\]
+Moreover, $(A,\lambda_\epsilon)$ and $(A,\lambda_{\epsilon'})$ are isomorphic
+as polarized abelian varieties over~$k$ if and only if
+\[
+    \epsilon'=u^2\epsilon
+    \quad\text{for some }u\in\calO_F^\times.
+\]
+Thus the polarized $k$-isomorphism classes of $k$-defined principal
+polarizations on~$A$ are in bijection with
+\[
+    \calO_F^{\times,+}/(\calO_F^\times)^2.
+\]
+
+If the reference polarization is replaced by
+$\lambda_\eta=\lambda\circ\iota(\eta)$, with
+$\eta\in\calO_F^{\times,+}$, then
+\[
+    x^{\dagger_{\lambda_\eta}}
+      =\iota(\eta)^{-1}x^{\dagger_\lambda}\iota(\eta)
+      \qquad\text{for every }x\in\End_{\kbar}^{0}(A).
+\]
+Both Rosati involutions restrict to the identity on
+$\End_k^0(A)=\iota(F)$.  No equality
+$\End_{\kbar}(A)=\iota(\calO_F)$ is asserted here, and the proposition does
+not classify polarizations over~$\kbar$ arising from additional geometric
+endomorphisms.
+\end{proposition}
+
+\begin{proof}
+Since~$F$ is totally real, $\dagger_\lambda$ restricts to the identity on
+$\iota(F)$.  The classification and its base-field isomorphism relation are
+the real quadratic specialization of
+\cite[Thm.~2.10]{GonzalezGuardiaRotger2005}.
+
+Indeed, $\operatorname{Aut}_k(A)=\iota(\calO_F^\times)$, and pullback by
+$\iota(u)$ sends $\lambda_\epsilon$ to $\lambda_{u^2\epsilon}$.  The displayed
+Rosati formula follows directly from the definition of the Rosati involution
+and $\lambda_\eta=\lambda\circ\iota(\eta)$.
+\end{proof}
 
